# 012 — Multitasking, independent instances and foreground — done

- Type: feature
- Priority: high
- Status: done (2026-09-18)
- Completion date: 2026-09-18
- Basis: working ELF loading, PIT/IDT, waiting without a busy loop, and three applications.

## Task statement

Separate a program (ELF file) from its instance (a task with a unique PID).
Allow different programs and several instances of the same program to run simultaneously,
while keeping the console responsive and the load low when there is no work.

Commands:

- `run <name>` — a new instance in the foreground; `boot` — shorthand for `run app`.
- `run <name> &` — a new instance in the background; the console stays available.
- `ps` — PID, name, state, foreground/background of the running tasks.
- `kill <id>` — terminate only the selected instance and free its resources.
- `fg <id>` — bring an existing instance to the foreground without restarting it.
- `Ctrl+Z` — return the console, leaving the current program running in the background.
- `Esc` — terminate the foreground program normally.

## Implementation

A scheduler on a single BSP with timer-driven switching and sleeping of individual tasks.
Each instance gets its own stack, loaded/relocated ELF
(including `.data` and `.bss`), syscall mailbox, screen and input queue.
Only the foreground instance receives the keyboard and is shown on the physical screen;
background programs keep computing and updating their own screens.
When all tasks are asleep, the processor uses `HLT`.
Finished tasks are freed from a different stack.

The first stage keeps ring 0 and a shared address space: hardware memory
isolation and SMP on four cores are separate future tasks.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Two `app` instances have independent global counters and stacks.
- [x] Several applications make progress simultaneously with a responsive console.
- [x] `ps`, `kill`, `fg`, foreground/background and `Ctrl+Z` work via UART and PS/2.
- [x] `fg` preserves the PID, state and screen contents of the selected instance.
- [x] Terminating one copy does not affect another; repeated starts free memory.
- [x] Invalid names/PIDs, task table overflow and out-of-memory produce errors.
- [x] The timer preempts a program that does not call wait.
- [x] `HLT` runs while idle; `heap` keeps working.
- [x] The build, load/scheduling checks and the QEMU integration check pass.
- [x] README updated; this task moved to `issues-done/*.done` after verification.

## Result

Implemented a preemptive round-robin scheduler on the BSP that saves registers and
x87/SSE state. Limit — 8 simultaneously running programs, monotonic PIDs, separate
64 KiB stacks, ELF images, ABI/mailbox, input queues and screen buffers.
UEFI keeps the original ELF files and reserves 64 MiB for the runtime heap.
Each launch copies the segments, zeroes `.bss` and applies relocations anew.

Added `run <name> [&]`, `ps`, `kill <id>`, `fg <id>`, `Ctrl+Z` and `logs <id>`.
The last command reads and clears a per-task log (the last 4096 bytes).
Foreground additionally prints to UART with the PID; background keeps its output in
the log without disturbing the console. Applications show Ctrl+Z/Esc hints.

The screen and input belong only to the selected instance; the others keep
running. `fg` preserves state. `kill` and returning from an application free
resources from the shell's stack. On a creation error, partial allocations are rolled back.
When no programs are ready, the shell executes `HLT`.

## Verification

- Full offline release build of all five ELF/UEFI components: no warnings.
- `tests/runtime.rs`: 14 tests passed — real ELF copies, independent `.bss`,
  relocations, corrupted headers/segments, task wake-up/selection, RTC.
- `tests/qemu_smoke.py`: three QEMU scenarios with 4 vCPUs passed:
  - normal: two `app`, two `clock` and `app2` simultaneously; progress of each task;
    `fg`/Ctrl+Z/Esc; `run`/`fg`/`kill` via PS/2 (including Shift+7 for `&`);
    invalid PIDs/names, eight tasks and rejection of the ninth, ten repeated launches;
    the heap returns exactly to its baseline value after cleanup; CPU0 `HLT=1`.
    The framebuffer was also checked: changing the colour of PID 3 does not change the colour of PID 4, a repeated `fg 3`
    preserves the colour; a screenshot of the foreground clock contains actually rendered digits.
  - memory: an ELF with an enlarged `.bss` drives the heap to out-of-memory before the
    table limit; a failed launch does not change the used memory, the remaining tasks keep running,
    freeing returns the heap to its original state, after which a new launch is possible.
  - busy: a test ELF runs an infinite loop after a single syscall
    without waiting, keeping a value in XMM0; the timer preempts it, the clock and the shell
    respond, `kill` terminates the task, memory is freed.
- `git diff --check` and the build script syntax: no errors.
- The package in `usb_root/` was rebuilt; the test ELFs are not part of the regular build.

## Files

- `kernel/src/scheduler.rs`, `context.rs`, `task_state.rs` — tasks and switching.
- `kernel/src/elf.rs`, `memory.rs` — loading instances and freeing resources.
- `kernel/src/input.rs`, `main.rs`, `interrupts.rs` — input, commands, timer.
- `common/abi.rs`, `common/wait.rs`, `bootloader/src/main.rs` — ABI and RAM/ELF preparation.
- `tests/runtime.rs`, `tests/qemu_smoke.py`, `tests/busy_app.rs` — checks.
- `README.md` — commands, limitations and reproducible test runs.

The limitations of this stage remain: ring 0, a shared address space, execution on
a single BSP; SMP, hardware memory protection, own GDT/TSS/IST and AVX context
are not part of this task.

## Audit (2026-10-04)

Done. Corrections: the host-test count changed (scheduler-policy and RTC tests were removed with their modules); `common/wait.rs` moved into `libmind`; the commands moved to the ring-3 shell (021).
